The baseline is already a single plaintext add, with no multiplications, rotations, rescales or alignments, so there is little left to cut. The 0.45 ms is mostly the cost of encoding the scalar and adding it.

I considered two alternatives, and neither looks like a clear win:
- Adding the scalar to the ciphertext through a different formulation, such as `x0 - (-s)`. That would need plaintext arithmetic, which is not allowed, and it would not change the operation count.
- Computing the sum some other way. Any such route introduces extra operations, which would only make it slower.

I'm resubmitting the baseline circuit unchanged. It cannot win under the judging rules because its circuit matches the baseline's, but I don't see a valid circuit with fewer operations.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext s("s");
  Ciphertext y = x0 + s;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The only remaining cost is the plaintext add, which includes encoding the scalar. A different circuit has to cut that cost, and I see one option.

The plaintext `s` is a scalar, so it may be encoded as a single value broadcast to all slots. Adding it as `s + x0` would be normalized to the same circuit as the baseline. Any attempt to avoid the encoding would need constants or plaintext arithmetic, which the rules forbid.

One structurally different variant is `(x0 + s) + x0 - x0`, but that adds operations and is strictly slower. I'm trying a variant that computes `x0 - (x0 - s)`. It is mathematically equal to `x0 + s` but uses two plaintext/ciphertext subtractions, so it will likely be slower. It is still a distinct circuit, so I'm submitting it to see how the measurement behaves.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext s("s");
  Ciphertext t = x0 - s;
  Ciphertext y = x0 - t;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The failure is explained by the circuit. `x0 - (x0 - s)` makes the ciphertext part cancel to zero, so SEAL reports a transparent ciphertext. That was a poor idea, and I should not have expected it to be faster.

The baseline is a single add with one plaintext encoding, and I don't see a valid circuit with fewer operations. The one other option is to change how the scalar is added, without cancelling the ciphertext:

- `y = s + x0` is normalized to the same circuit as the baseline.
- `y = x0 - (-s)` needs plaintext negation, which is not allowed.
- Subtracting a negated ciphertext, `x0 - (-x0 - s)`, doubles x0 and does not give x0 + s.

I'm trying `(x0 + x0) + s` minus `x0`. It computes 2·x0 + s − x0 = x0 + s, and it never cancels to a transparent ciphertext. It has three adds instead of one, so I expect it to be slower. It is a different circuit that stays valid, and it shows whether the measurement can differ at all.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext s("s");
  Ciphertext d = x0 + x0;
  Ciphertext t = d + s;
  Ciphertext y = t - x0;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The 1.47 ms result confirms that extra adds only slow things down. The baseline is one ciphertext-plaintext add, and every other form I can build from the allowed operators has at least as many operations. I don't see a circuit with fewer operations than one add, so I don't expect any remaining attempt to beat the baseline.

Any circuit that differs from the baseline has to add operations, because the output depends on both inputs and so needs at least one operation. A cheaper single operation does not exist. A valid circuit that is also faster is therefore unlikely.

I'm submitting the baseline kernel again as the best known answer.

